  "tests": "eval/tests/test_b4w_slice.py::B4WFixtureTests, ::B4WFixtureRefusalTests, ::B4WWALEncodingTests, ::B4WCrashInjectionTests, ::B4WRecoveryTests, ::B4WScoringTests, ::B4WGateTests, ::B4WMutationTests, ::B4WBookkeepingTests",
  "test_counts": {
    "b4w_slice_tests": 74,
    "whole_suite": 443,
    "selftest_checks": 17,
    "x10_regressions": 6
  },
  "harness_tests": "python3 -m unittest discover -s eval/tests -t . -> 443 tests OK; python3 -m eval.harness selftest -> 17/17 checks passed; python3 -m eval.harness x10 --block B4W -> 6/6 regressions caught by their declared gate",
  "solver_note": "every run uses the deterministic hand-written 'stub' reference profile. No model, provider, parameter count, hardware or network call is involved. The solver sees the instruction, the records, the planted history and the operations; it learns where the process died only by dying there.",
  "what_this_shows": [
    "a committed record survives a simulated process death: after the restart and the final recovery the store holds exactly the gold durable set, with one line per record",
    "a write interrupted before its commit marker leaves nothing behind: no record in the store and no residue line after recovery, so 'uncommitted' and 'committed' are separated on disk rather than in a report",
    "the damage class comes from the bytes: a truncated frame reads as torn, a frame with a flipped payload byte reads as corrupt, and the reference program reports what recovery found rather than what the fixture says",
    "repair is bounded: a torn or corrupt tail is cut back to the last verifiable frame and the log is appendable again, while damage that precedes the frames the run wrote stops the replay and the verifiable frames after it are refused (b4w-008)",
    "the crash point is the evaluator's: six tasks declare candidate stages, the stage and the in-operation offset come from sha256(task:salt:attempt), all six stages are exercised across the suite, and the fixture is refused if its gold would depend on which candidate is picked",
    "the six planted regressions are separable: each fails the gate that owns its failure direction, at the tasks it damages, with the clean run passing all seven gates",
    "cleanup is real: the tests assert no store directory survives a run, that a marker file outside the store is untouched, and that the store refuses a foreign directory name, a symlinked root and a path with '..' in it"
  ],
  "what_this_does_not_show": [
    "that anything will be durable in a real system: the process dies because a Python function raises at a chosen byte, there is no kernel, no page cache, no power loss, no firmware reordering and no fsync semantics beyond flushing the file object the harness opened",
    "how a real storage engine recovers: no real checksum or key management, no encryption at rest, no replication, no consensus, no real WAL format",
    "anything about performance: write amplification, fsync cost and recovery time are not measured",
    "any statistical rate: ten scenarios, one deterministic execution each, seven simulated crashes over six stages, 603 bytes truncated - these are scenarios, not samples, and no confidence interval may be computed from them",
    "any product capability: the store holds invented records, the solver is a hand-written program, C-02-AC3 stays slice_built, no capability is marked implemented or validated, and G-O1 stays blocked"
  ],
  "correlated_failures": [
    "fewer than one gate fires per mutant: accept_torn_entry also trips uncommitted_write_zero and recovery_complete_zero (keeping an unverifiable write really does leave a record the log never committed), and claim_recovery_complete_with_partial_write also trips uncommitted_write_zero. Each mutant is still caught by its declared gate, and the counters stay separable: the x10 table names which gates fired for each.",
    "false_successes_zero and success_drop fire alongside the expected gate on every mutant; they are aggregate companions, not family-specific signals, and are not used here to argue isolation"
  ],
  "remaining_gaps": [
    "no real crash and no real store: the byte-level mechanism is a Python exception",
    "no concurrent writers, no multi-process access, no log segmentation or checkpointing",
    "no model is involved, so nothing here measures how a real agent or engine behaves after an interrupted write",
    "Q18 (people-database fields) stays open; retention and legal hold are untouched",
    "the durability of the future product store is unmeasured, because there is no product store and no storage decision (G-O1 blocked)"
  ],
  "reproduce": [
    "python3 -m unittest discover -s eval/tests -t .",
    "python3 -m eval.harness selftest",
    "python3 -m eval.harness run --block B4W",
    "python3 -m eval.harness x10 --block B4W --report eval/results/x10-report-m5-b4w-durability.json",
    "python3 -m eval.harness baseline-freeze --block B4W --partial --name baseline-m5-b4w-durability.json --note 'B4W durability slice (2026-Q4.1-m5.7)'"
  ],
  "what_was_learned": [
    "the four-state audit from the restore slice transfers to a log-structured store with only one change of meaning: 'partially applied' becomes a record line without a commit marker, and the third invariant - that the record file equals the committed set after recovery - is what catches a recovery that trusts a torn write or skips its own rebuild",
    "appending after a torn frame silently corrupts the log for every later read: the repair step (cut back to the last verifiable frame before writing again) is not optional, and without it the damage looked like a checksum failure in a much later entry",
    "an interruption can be reported without leaking where it happened, provided the fixture declares candidates and the evaluator picks one deterministically: the same gold has to hold for every candidate, which is also the cheapest way to catch a fixture that accidentally encodes its own answer",
    "'recovery complete' had to become a claim the scorer checks against the bytes, because a solver can rebuild the log, skip the record file and still be able to describe the committed set correctly"
  ],
  "what_remains_uncertain": [
    "whether these seven counters cover the ways a real engine loses data - the fixture exercises what we thought of, and a real crash has more shapes (partial pages, reordered writes, a log that survives while the data file does not)",
    "where the boundary is between 'repair the tail' and 'refuse the log': this slice draws it at the last verifiable frame, but a real engine also weighs how much is being discarded",
    "how any of this maps onto the product's eventual store, which does not exist yet and must not be chosen on the strength of a synthetic slice",
    "whether the reference recovery would pass a real durability test (fault injection at the filesystem level, kill -9 under load, power-cut emulation) - nothing here simulates those"
  ],
  "next_research_step": "take the same seven counters and the same hidden-schedule discipline to a second, structurally different recovery path - for example a checkpointed store where the log is truncated on a schedule - so that a passing result cannot be an artefact of one log layout, and only then consider whether any of this constrains a real storage decision. G-O1 stays blocked and no model, provider or storage product is selected."
}
